  "description": "Implement grouped-query attention by sharing each key/value head across a group of query heads.",
  "theory": "## What Is Grouped Query Attention?\n\nScaled Dot-Product and Multi-Head Attention (Vaswani et al., 2017) compute context-aware token representations by routing information across positions according to pairwise query-key compatibility scores.\n\nImplement grouped-query attention by sharing each key/value head across a group of query heads.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulation & Derivation\n\nScaling dot products by $1 / \\sqrt{d_k}$ prevents the variance of $\\mathbf{q}^T \\mathbf{k}$ from growing with embedding dimension $d_k$, keeping softmax inputs out of flat saturation regions where gradients vanish.\n\n---\n\n## Key Properties & Conceptual Intuition\n\n- Implement the mathematical or algorithmic behavior described above.\n- Match the function signature and output structure in starterCode.\n- **Related Topics in This Track:** **Causal Masked Attention**, **Multi-Head Attention**, **Scaled Dot-Product Attention**.\n\n---\n\n## Numerical Stability & Edge Cases\n\nWhen evaluating exponentials over raw scores or logits, shifting by the maximum value prevents floating-point overflow without changing normalized ratios:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{softmax}(z_i) = \\frac{\\exp(z_i - \\max_k z_k)}{\\sum_{j} \\exp(z_j - \\max_k z_k)}\n$$\n\nWhen dividing by a variance, norm, or sample count that may vanish on constant or degenerate inputs, guard the denominator with an additive $\\varepsilon$ or explicit zero check:\n\n$$\n\\text{scale} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 + \\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\n- **Boundary & Shape Consistency:** Ensure 1D vectors, 2D matrices, and single-element inputs preserve their expected output dimensions and rounding precision.\n\n---\n\n## Computational Complexity\n\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ is the total number of input elements, executing in a single vectorized pass.\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ to hold the output array (or $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ auxiliary working memory beyond the result).",
